You are given a map of a region as an `N x M` grid. A cell marked `1` is **buildable** (a solar power plant can be built on it) and a cell marked `0` is not.

Place **two square-shaped power plants** on the map so that:

- both squares have the **same side length** `s`;
- every cell inside each square is buildable;
- the two squares do **not share any cell**;
- a single cell (side length `1`) counts as a valid square.

Squares are aligned with the grid. Find the **maximum possible side length** `s` for which two such squares exist. If it is impossible to place two squares at all (even of side `1`), print `0`.

